## Migration Order

| Step | Work | Why It Comes Here | Exit Criteria |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Unify Hub and device product paths under `easynet-daemon`. | This establishes one policy owner before any public daemon API is stabilized. | Hub mode no longer starts raw `axon-runtime` as the product path. Runtime state no longer needs `AxonBridge` for product Hub. |
| 2 | Promote daemon Invocation transport to first-class daemon API. | Backend and bindings need a stable daemon Invocation endpoint before JSON control can be demoted. | daemon Invocation transport is named and documented as the main call surface; unary, stream, and bidi are all proven. |
| 3 | Add daemon SDK lifecycle and client APIs. | After Hub/device unification, public API can safely represent the actual daemon abstraction. | `DaemonConfig`, `DaemonHandle`, `DaemonClient`, endpoint discovery, status, stop, and invoke APIs exist in `libeasynet_cli`. |
| 4 | Replace ability+args FFI with complete Invocation FFI. | Receipt-chain integrity requires all seven fields at public binding boundaries. | C ABI has unary, stream, and bidi complete Invocation entry points; old ability+args APIs are deprecated or compatibility-only. |
| 5 | Complete JSON control caller inventory. | Demotion risk depends on actual callers, not intent. | Every Invoke, Subscribe, and OpenBidi caller is classified as backend, language binding, CLI command, boot/status path, daemon internal, or test. |
| 6 | Demote JSON control. | Once product ability callers have daemon Invocation replacements, JSON control can return to control-plane duties. | JSON control is limited to boot, status, lifecycle, diagnostics, and explicitly non-product control flows. |
| 7 | Remove or shrink CLI-owned Invocation semantics. | After public callers use Axon Invocation, CLI should not retain a second semantic model. | CLI runtime Invocation is deleted or reduced to an adapter with no canonical signing or receipt authority. |

